# Authored learning specifications for #549. No application execution is claimed.
Feature: Use a checked draft responsibly and improve a repeatable workflow

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-001 — Keep useful planning facts without the contact sheet
    Given E4 has six helpers, forty packets, one hour and private phone fields
    And the task is an invented rough planning exercise
    When the learner chooses the input in L4-E-P
    Then the useful sample retains the counts, time limit and unknown arrangements
    And it excludes the contact sheet and phone numbers
    And removing names alone is not described as permission for public sharing

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-002 — Create a new practice problem instead of a renamed private complaint
    Given W4 contains a restricted internal client complaint
    When the learner prepares a fictional action-table exercise
    Then the worked example uses a different library situation, two examples and Monday
    And it preserves the task-owner-date structure rather than the complaint's wording
    And the result is identified as a new practice problem, not a faithful source summary

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-003 — Preserve a testable behavior without carrying credentials
    Given T4's original log contains a token and a customer's address
    And the behavior to practise is a blank-message explanation
    When the learner chooses the useful sample in L4-T-P
    Then it contains invented input, the expected behavior and not-run status
    And neither the original log nor its credential is needed
    And a screenshot is not treated as removal of information

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-004 — Use the actual limited sharing permission
    Given L4-E-X allows ten helpers and Saturday in a public gardening notice
    And helper contact details are excluded from that notice
    When the learner writes the notice and names its audience
    Then those two allowed facts can be used for the public audience
    And contact details and an invented address or start time are absent
    And the learner does not invent a broader permission or an unnecessary blanket prohibition

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-005 — Check time and simultaneous staffing separately
    Given E5 has six helpers for sixty minutes
    And sequential draft slots take ten, forty-five and ten minutes with two, seven and three helpers
    When the learner checks the plan
    Then they identify sixty-five total minutes and a seven-helper peak as separate conflicts
    And the worked repair uses ten, forty and ten minutes with a six-helper peak
    And passing these numerical checks does not establish practical feasibility

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-006 — Keep an unsupported commitment unverified when the source is missing
    Given the draft in L5-W-X assigns Jo an announcement on Friday
    And the source cannot be opened and no trusted copy is retained
    When the learner records a workflow decision
    Then the commitment remains unverified rather than confirmed or declared false
    And the recovery step retrieves the source or asks for confirmation of the owner and date
    And other retained useful work is not discarded

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-007 — Recover from a drafting failure without a fabricated result
    Given T5 has a blank-message rule and no executed tests
    When the drafting tool stops before the plan is complete
    Then the learner can draft manually or retry from the retained source
    And the plan's execution status remains not run

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-008 — Give feedback that identifies the next useful change
    Given E6 states six helpers and no start time
    And draft version 1 says eight helpers at ten in the morning
    When the learner writes a practice feedback note
    Then it identifies both mismatches and their source evidence
    And it suggests six helpers and a time to be confirmed
    And its limit does not claim a real event check or independent assessment

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-009 — Decline unsupported advice with a source-based reason
    Given W6 gives Thursday as the trial date and no survey deadline
    And feedback suggests making Mina's survey due Thursday
    When the learner decides how to respond
    Then they may keep the survey deadline not specified
    And they explain the difference between trial date and survey deadline
    And they ask whether another source establishes the proposed deadline

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-010 — Recognise a correction already made in a newer draft
    Given T6 has no executed tests and no specified message length limit
    And draft version 1 says passed and five hundred characters
    And draft version 2 already says not run and unspecified
    When feedback on version 1 requests those corrections
    Then the learner can record already addressed in version 2 with the matching changes
    And the earlier comment is not relabelled as a review or approval of version 2

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-011 — Match the feedback decision to a fresh source and version
    Given L6-E-X supplies nine readers and no room
    And L6-W-X already corrects the agenda owner in version 2
    And L6-T-X claims an unexecuted test passed
    When the learner evaluates the three sample feedback situations
    Then the reading-group invention is declined or clarified with evidence
    And the agenda-owner comment is recorded as already addressed
    And the test result is revised to separate expectation from not-run status

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-012 — Assemble a complete foundation practice record
    Given the learner chooses CAP-E, CAP-W or CAP-T
    When they complete the six-step practice
    Then the record keeps the source, task, request, claim check and corrected answer together
    And it states the input and audience scope, workflow and recovery step
    And it includes practice feedback, its source version and a reasoned revision decision
    And unfinished criteria point back to a lesson rather than producing a mastery certificate

  Scenario: APPLIED-LEARN-013 — Record interrupted execution separately from expected behavior
    Given L5-T-X requires PDFs no larger than two megabytes
    And a three-megabyte PDF rejection test stopped before any response was observed
    When the learner records the test case
    Then rejection is an expected result derived from the size rule
    And execution is interrupted or unconfirmed rather than passed or failed
    And the retained case can be rerun when the test setup is usable
